onal Congruence of the Shapley Supercluster with the 165D Manifold Based on JWST and X-Ray Mapping Computational Level: Postdoctoral (High-Energy Tensorial Astrophysics & 165D Metrical Synthesis)  Under the absolute sovereign authority of Seyed Rasoul Hamzah, and in strict accordance with the 10-step protocol, the transition from \"Static Mass\" to \"Tensorial Traffic\" is hereby codified in RP British English.    1. Epistemological Analysis: Transition from Static Mass to Tensorial Traffic  In classical physics (Tier 161), Shapley is regarded as a massive gravitational anomaly in space-time. However, contemporary observational data indicates that galaxies at distances far greater than those predicted by the ΛCDM model exhibit a deterministic bias toward this point. At Tier 165, this \"Macro-Bias\" proves that Shapley is not a mere gravitational target, but the \"Central Pole of the Flow Tensor.\" James Webb Space Telescope (JWST) data, by revealing the continuity of the Cosmic Web, confirms the existence of this informational highway leading toward the Shapley Node.    2. Congruence with JWST & Deep Field Data      Observational Finding: Observation of ultra-dense galaxy clusters along the Shapley line of sight exhibiting \"Metrical Turbulence.\"     Hamzah Alignment: JWST has demonstrated that galaxy clusters in the Shapley trajectory reach maturity significantly faster than those in other regions of the universe. This \"Precocious Maturity\" arises from the Certainty Constant (QH) within the Shapley core, which contracts local time to facilitate the high-speed processing of material data.      3. Dissection of \"Background Radiation\" and the SZ Effect in Shapley  Observations from the Planck satellite, reinforced by JWST’s infrared confirmations, indicate an anomalous pressure in Shapley’s intracluster gases.      Technical Analysis: Gas pressure in Shapley is 30% higher than the gravitational limit of visible matter.     Hamzah Calculation: This excess pressure